<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ii Naomasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1561">1561</a>–<a href="/years/1602">1602</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ii Naomasa was one of the four great generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, commander of the red-armoured troops of the Ii. Wounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, he received the former lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> at Sawayama. His family moved the seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a> and remained among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s most trusted houses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa.jpg" alt="Ii Naomasa" width="320" height="636" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ii_Naomasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Naomasa was born in Hōda Village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>; his childhood names were Toramatsu and later Manchiyo. The Ii, like the Tokugawa, had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a> fell at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okehazama">Battle of Okehazama</a> in 1560 his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujizane">Imagawa Ujizane</a> had Naomasa's father, Ii Naochika, falsely convicted of treason and killed. Ii Naotora took over the clan and became the boy's guardian. According to the Ii family biography, in 1574 Naotora and the priest Nankei Zuimon resolved to place him in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Naomasa first fought in 1576, against Takeda Katsuyori's forces at Shibahara. He received a domain of 10,000 koku in 1578 and 20,000 in 1580, and old Ii vassals who had fled to the mountains returned to serve him. In 1582 he took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> who had served Tsuchiya Masatsugu, one of the Twenty-Four Generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, and in the Tenshō-Jingo War of that year he handled some 67 letters that brought local lords of Shinano over to the Tokugawa. These former Takeda men formed his red-armoured troops.</p><p>At the Battle of Nagakute on 9 April 1584 he led about 3,000 men on the Tokugawa left; his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> volleys broke repeated charges until Mori Nagayoshi was shot dead, and his conduct drew praise from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> on the other side. When peace was made, Hideyoshi's mother, Ōmandokoro, stayed with him as a guarantee of the alliance. After Hideyoshi's death in 1598 he drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-yoshitaka">Kuroda Yoshitaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> into a pact that swayed other commanders toward the Tokugawa, and he built Takasaki Castle as his seat. In 1600 he led the forces on the Tokaido with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s representative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His early death in 1602 has been widely blamed on the wound he received at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>. Contemporaries described him as handsome and small, but in battle he wore red armour and a horned helmet, which earned him the name Aka-oni, red demon, and his harsh discipline earned him another, Hitokiri Hyōbu. His sons Naotsugu and Naotaka succeeded him. The historian Hiroko Noda sees his rise as the start of the Ii as a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> house, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. In 1917 Emperor Taishō granted him the junior third rank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ii Naomasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ii Naomasa</b>, one of Tokugawa Ieyasu's most trusted generals and leader of the red-armoured troops, was given the former lands of Ishida Mitsunari at Sawayama after Sekigahara in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/hikone-domain">Hikone Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.96,137.95&amp;year=1587" aria-label="Tōtōmi Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8047 899 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ii Naomasa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8497 1169h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8497 1169h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.96,137.95&amp;year=1587">What was here in 1587? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
